&lt;div class="sectionbody"&gt;
&lt;div class="paragraph abstract-title"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abstract&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="paragraph"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This article describes the release engineering process of the FreeBSD Project.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;hr/&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="sect1"&gt;
&lt;h2 id="introduction"&gt;1. Introduction to the FreeBSD Release Engineering Process&lt;a class="anchor" href="#introduction"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;div class="sectionbody"&gt;
&lt;div class="paragraph"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Development of FreeBSD has a very specific workflow.
In general, all changes to the FreeBSD base system are committed to the main branch, which reflects the top of the source tree.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="paragraph"&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After a reasonable testing period, changes can then be merged to the stable/ branches.
The default minimum timeframe before merging to stable/ branches is three (3) days.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>